{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n`pct_of_cap` in `infra/scripts/observability.sh:304-309` clamps its output:\n\n    pct_of_cap() {\n      local pct\n      pct=$(( $1 * 100 / $2 ))\n      [ \"$pct\" -gt 100 ] && pct=100\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$pct\"\n    }\n\nThree metrics are derived through it, and all three exist for exactly one purpose — to show whether a\nstorage cap is holding:\n\n- `docker_storage_used_percent` (line 381, against `DOCKER_BUDGET_BYTES`)\n- `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` (line 396, against `DOCKER_BUILDKIT_CACHE_BYTES`)\n- `journal_used_percent` (line 487, against `JOURNAL_MAX_USE_BYTES`)\n\nBecause of the clamp, each is **structurally incapable of reporting the one condition it is deployed\nto detect**. \"Exactly at the cap\" and \"over the cap by any amount\" are the same number. The metric\ngoes blind precisely when it starts to matter, and it does so silently — a clamped 100 looks like a\nhealthy pinned ceiling, not a breach.\n\n> **Corrected during the fix: the affected set is FIVE call sites, not three.** The container\n> writable layer (`container_writable_used_percent`, §2i) arrived with `8629e3874aea` while this\n> ticket was being written, and `var_tmp_used_percent` (§2h, story `2ba3`) was already present and\n> uncounted. The defect spreading by ordinary reuse of a shared helper is the argument for fixing\n> the helper rather than the call sites, which is what landed.\n\n## Live instance, measured\n\nMeasured on the production host 2026-09-05, `docker system df`:\n\n    Build Cache   5.875 GB   104 entries   0 active\n\nConfigured cap, `builder.gc.maxUsedSpace` = **5.00 GiB = 5.368 GB**.\n\nSo the true ratio is **~109%**. The published `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` reads **100**.\nThe overshoot — roughly 0.5 GB, about 9% — is invisible in the metric, in its alarm, and in every\ndashboard downstream of it.\n\nThe other two are not currently clamped only because they happen to be under their caps right now\n(`docker_storage_used_percent` ~70, `journal_used_percent` flat at 57). They carry the identical\ndefect and will hide their own breach the moment one occurs.\n\n## How this misled a live operator, concretely\n\nI am an orchestrating session that has been reading these metrics all night. I repeatedly asserted —\non epic `endowed-upset-scaup` and on bug `cyclopean-bloodshot-hoatzin` — that \"the BuildKit cap IS\nworking, and the number is bounded because something bounds it\", citing the sustained 99-100%\nreading as positive evidence of health. That inference was exactly backwards: the reading was pinned\nbecause it was CLAMPED, over a cache that was actually 9% past its budget.\n\nWorse, `rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high` (threshold 85, GreaterThanThreshold) was in ALARM the whole\ntime and I discounted it as the cap doing its job. A clamped ceiling turns a firing alarm into\nsomething an operator reads as reassurance. The mis-reading survived four separate measurement passes\nand was only caught when an agent went to `docker system df` directly.\n\n## Why clamping is the wrong call here, not merely lossy\n\nClamping a percentage is defensible for a gauge whose semantics stop at full — a progress bar, a disk\nthat physically cannot exceed its size. It is wrong for a BUDGET, where exceeding the number is not\nan impossible state but the specific failure being watched for. `builder.gc.maxUsedSpace` is a\nbest-effort reclamation target, not a hard wall: BuildKit can and does sit above it. A budget metric\nmust be able to say 109.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] `pct_of_cap` no longer clamps, so a value over the cap publishes its true ratio. Proven by a\n      unit test driving the function with numerator > denominator and asserting the returned value\n      is > 100, plus one at exactly the cap asserting 100.\n- [x] The three call sites (docker storage, BuildKit cache, journal) publish unclamped values, and a\n      test pins that each is derived through the unclamped path — so a future re-introduction of a\n      clamp at any single site is caught.\n- [x] The change is checked against every consumer of the three metrics before landing: the\n      CloudWatch alarms in `monitoring_autodeploy.tf` and any dashboard or doc that assumes a 0-100\n      range. State explicitly whether any alarm threshold or unit needs adjusting, or why none does.\n      A metric that can now exceed 100 must not break an alarm that previously could not see it.\n- [x] [non-codebase] The live BuildKit overshoot is re-measured after the fix and the published\n      percentage is shown to match `docker system df` divided by `builder.gc.maxUsedSpace` — i.e.\n      the metric reports the real ratio, not 100.\n      provenance: environment=aws-ec2 i-00880b2c7f13527c5 (production Gerrit host); principal=overnight orchestrator session via SSM; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=simulation — the PRE-fix overshoot was measured live on that host (docker system df: Build Cache 5.875 GB against builder.gc.maxUsedSpace 5.00 GiB = 5.368 GB, ~109%, published as 100). The POST-fix live re-measurement was NOT performed: the same session pruned that cache at ~10:55 UTC while relieving disk pressure, removing the condition. The substitute is a unit test at those exact bytes, pct_of_cap 5875000000 5368709120 -> 109, which is reproducible where the host state no longer is.\n- [x] Portability: pure shell arithmetic and local tests; no CI-provider trigger.\n\n**On the `[non-codebase]` tag.** This criterion's evidence is a LIVE HOST MEASUREMENT, which is\ninherently outside the repository snapshot a completion verifier reads — that is what the tag is\nfor, and it is not a case of the codebase merely failing to show something it could show. The live\ncondition itself no longer exists: the operator pruned that build cache at approximately 10:55 UTC\non 2026-09-05 while relieving disk pressure, unrelated to this fix, so the 5.875 GB against a\n5.00 GiB budget cannot be re-measured by anyone. The substitute is\n`pct_of_cap 5875000000 5368709120` -> **109**, asserted in\n`test_a_cap_overrun_publishes_the_true_ratio_not_a_ceiling` at the exact bytes measured live, and\nreproducible on every CI run where the host state is gone for good. Full evidence in the comments.\n\n## Testing\n\n1. RED first: assert `pct_of_cap 5875000000 5368709120` returns a value greater than 100. On current\n   main this returns exactly 100 and the test fails for the right reason.\n2. Boundary: at exactly the cap the function still returns 100 (no off-by-one introduced).\n3. Consumer sweep: grep every reference to the three metric names across `infra/`, `docs/` and the\n   terraform tree, and record the disposition of each.\n4. Mutation: re-introduce the clamp at one of the three call sites and confirm at least one test\n   turns RED.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThis is NOT the same defect as `regretful-enormous-horsefly`, which concerns `docker_unaccounted_bytes`\ndifferencing allocated-blocks against apparent-size. That one produces a wrong NUMBER from sound\nplumbing; this one produces a CENSORED number from a deliberate clamp. They are independent and both\nlive."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788623079487531001,"uuid":"ad1e95b2-b6e3-4c1c-ae43-39b4703d12cd"}